Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 08:53:25 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 038/154] feat: bring back the command bar as an HTML surface
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The palette has been a no-op since the Swift cut: OpenCommandBar showed a
widget nothing constructed, so all 47 call sites reached nothing. This
rebuilds it on the sidebar's shape - a WebUI surface fed from the browser
process - and leaves those call sites untouched.

- /commandbar route: commandbar.js and commandbar.css, no innerHTML and no
  Trusted Types policy, with 13 unit tests behind make webui-test.
- Five chrome.send names in kSurfaceMessages. An unregistered name kills the
  browser process, so the JS suite reads the list from the header.
- atrium_command_bar_matches: which omnibox string goes on which line, and
  the wire snapshot. Portable C++, 7 gtest cases.
- CommandBarWidget hosts a WebView instead of a Swift NSView, and lost its
  AtriumHost and UIBridge parameters.
- AutocompleteBridge pushes finished passes to the page and answers where a
  match points.

Six command_bar_connected() gates removed. They asked the dylib whether a
palette existed, which since the cut is never, so every trigger declined
before it reached the widget. The VM found that, not review.

Two more the VM found: accept resolved its URL after Close() had clobbered
the autocomplete result, and the WebView was never the focused view, so the
palette came up visible, key, and deaf to every keystroke.

Verified: scenario command-bar, 4 passed 0 failed, with
command_bar=0 in the same run - Cmd+T opens it, seven keystrokes each reach
the bridge, Escape closes it.

Signed-off-by: János Mikó <693558+janosmiko@users.noreply.github.com>
